## Statement

Let $R$ be a Noetherian commutative ring. Then the polynomial ring $R[x]$ is a
Noetherian commutative ring.

No hypothesis beyond Noetherianity is placed on $R$: it may have zero divisors,
and it may be the zero ring.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A Noetherian commutative ring $R$ and its polynomial ring $R[x]$.

[L1] For every commutative ring $R$, the coefficientwise addition and convolution multiplication make $R[x]$ a commutative ring, and the constant-polynomial map $R\to R[x]$ is an injective unital ring homomorphism ([[thm-polynomial-ring-is-a-commutative-ring]]).

[L2] Over a Noetherian commutative ring $R$, every ideal of $R[x]$ is generated by finitely many polynomials realising generators of its stage ideals up to a stabilisation degree; in particular every ideal of $R[x]$ is finitely generated ([[lem-polynomial-ideal-finite-generation]]).

[L3] A commutative ring whose ideals are all finitely generated is Noetherian. This is the choice-free implication $2\Rightarrow1$ of [[thm-noetherian-ring-ideal-characterisations]], established by the regular-module/ideal identification in its proof steps 1.1–3.1. Its dependent-choice assumption is used only to pass from ACC to the maximal condition; that implication is not used here.

## Proof

**Proof technique:** direct.

1.1 $R[x]$ is a commutative ring. [L1, given]

2.1 Every ideal of $R[x]$ is finitely generated, by the finite-generation lemma applied to the Noetherian ring $R$. [L2, step 1.1]

3.1 Apply only the choice-free finite-generation-to-Noetherian implication in [L3] to the commutative ring $R[x]$ from step 1.1 and the finite generation from step 2.1. Thus $R[x]$ is Noetherian; no passage through the DC-dependent maximal condition is made. [L3, step 1.1, step 2.1] ∎

## Remarks

- **The work is in the lemmas, and it is of three separate kinds.** The stage ideals and their ascent are [[lem-leading-coefficient-ideals]] and use no chain condition; the one degree-lowering step is [[lem-hilbert-basis-degree-cancellation]] and uses no chain condition either; the passage to a finite generating list is [[lem-polynomial-ideal-finite-generation]] and is where the Noetherian hypothesis is spent. Keeping them apart is what makes each checkable on its own.

- **No leading coefficient is inverted anywhere.** A reader who has seen the argument only over a field may expect a division step; over a general commutative ring the stage ideals carry that role, which is why they are ideals of $R$ rather than single elements.
