+
+## Statement
+
+Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $X$ be a scheme of finite type over $\mathbb C$
+and let $x\in X$ be a closed point with residue field $\mathbb C$. Choose an
+affine neighborhood of $x$ presented as
+$\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb C[w_1,\ldots,w_n]/I)$, with $n\ge1$, $I$ a
+finitely generated polynomial ideal contained in $(w_1,\ldots,w_n)$, and $x$
+the origin. Write
+$A=(\mathbb C[w_1,\ldots,w_n]_{(w_1,\ldots,w_n)})/I\mathbb C[w_1,\ldots,w_n]_{(w_1,\ldots,w_n)}$.
+On this chart, define the analytic local ring by extending the same ideal to
+holomorphic germs,
+$B=\mathbb C\{w_1,\ldots,w_n\}/I\mathbb C\{w_1,\ldots,w_n\}$; this quotient
+retains the given scheme structure, including nilpotents. For a local ring $R$
+with maximal ideal $\mathfrak m_R$, let
+$\widehat R=\varprojlim_{k\ge1}R/\mathfrak m_R^k$.
+
+The canonical local homomorphism $A\to B$, which sends each polynomial class
+to the same holomorphic germ, is injective and faithfully flat.
+Its induced map $\widehat A\to\widehat B$ is an isomorphism, with both
+completions canonically identified with
+$\mathbb C[[w_1,\ldots,w_n]]/I\mathbb C[[w_1,\ldots,w_n]]$. For every
+$A$-module $M$, the map $M\to M\otimes_A B$ is injective, and the quotient
+$B/A$ is a flat $A$-module. These are local-ring conclusions; no relative
+direct-image comparison is asserted.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Serre, local algebraic-to-holomorphic comparison and flat-pair appendix](https://www.college-de-france.fr/media/jean-pierre-serre/UPL1698785858825941763_Serre_GAGA.pdf)
+
+[Anschütz, analytification of locally finite-type schemes](https://janschuetz.perso.math.cnrs.fr/skripte/lecture_notes_etale_cohomology.pdf)
+
+[MIT OpenCourseWare, Lecture 22: GAGA](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-726-algebraic-geometry-spring-2009/1329d3923b9f2d55ec7a9ec8e9cb63e9_MIT18_726s09_lec22_gaga.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The Axiom of Choice, a finite-type complex scheme $X$, a closed $\mathbb C$-point $x$, and the local rings and canonical map specified in the statement.
+
+[A1] AC says that every family of nonempty sets has a choice function ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
+
+[F1] A scheme is locally an affine scheme; finite type over $\mathbb C$ means each point has an affine neighborhood whose coordinate algebra is a finitely generated $\mathbb C$-algebra, hence a quotient of a polynomial ring ([[def-scheme]], [[def-locally-finite-type-and-finite-type-morphism]], [[def-finite-type-and-module-finite-algebras]]).
+
+[F2] The polynomial ring over $\mathbb C$ is Noetherian, so every defining ideal in the chosen chart is finitely generated ([[thm-hilbert-basis-theorem]]).
+
+[F2a] A commutative ring is Noetherian exactly when all its ideals are finitely generated ([[thm-noetherian-ring-ideal-characterisations]]).
+
+[F3] For the same polynomial ideal, the algebraic and holomorphic local rings are Noetherian local rings, the map between them is local, and both completions are the same formal-series quotient ([[lem-qftx-gaga-algebraic-analytic-local-completions]]).
+
+[F4] For any Noetherian local ring, its completion map is faithfully flat; in particular the completion is flat and the map is a local ring homomorphism ([[thm-completion-of-a-noetherian-local-ring]], [[thm-flatness-of-noetherian-completion]]).
+
+[F5] A module is flat exactly when tensoring preserves injections ([[thm-flatness-criteria-by-injections-and-ideals]]). A faithfully flat ring map is a flat ring map for which tensoring detects exactness ([[def-flat-and-faithfully-flat-modules-and-ring-maps]]).
+
+[F6] For a flat map of commutative rings, faithful flatness is equivalent to every proper ideal extending to a proper ideal ([[thm-faithfully-flat-ring-map-characterisations]]).
+
+[F7] Given a projective resolution, a short exact sequence of right modules gives the natural long exact Tor sequence in the right-module variable ([[thm-long-exact-tor-sequence-in-the-right-module-variable]]).
+
+[F8] Under DC and supplied projective-resolution data, a right module is flat exactly when its first Tor with every left module vanishes ([[thm-a-right-module-is-flat-exactly-when-tor-one-with-every-left-module-vanishes]]).
+
+[F9] Given a set, an initial element, and a self-map, recursion on $\mathbb N$ supplies the unique sequence following that map ([[thm-recursion]]).
+
+[F10] The free module on a set has its standard basis, and any map from that basis to a module extends to a module homomorphism ([[def-free-module-on-a-set-and-standard-basis]], [[thm-universal-property-of-free-modules]]).
+
+## Proof
+
+**Proof technique:** direct.
+
+1.1 AC implies DC: for a nonempty set $Y$ and a serial relation $R\subseteq Y\times Y$, the family of successor sets $\{\{z\in Y:yRz\}:y\in Y\}$ consists of nonempty sets. [A1] gives a choice function on this family; composing it with the successor-set map gives a self-map $s:Y\to Y$ with $yRs(y)$. For any prescribed $y_0$, [F9] gives the sequence $y_{k+1}=s(y_k)$. For any module $N$, take the free module on its underlying set and map each basis element to the element it names; [F10] makes this a surjection. Repeat the same construction on each successive kernel, with [F9] organizing the recursion, to obtain a projective resolution. Thus the DC and resolution hypotheses of [F7] and [F8] hold. [A1, F7, F8, F9, F10, construct]
+
+1.2 By [F1] and [F2], around $x$ the scheme has the finite polynomial presentation in the statement, and its ideal $I$ is finitely generated. Apply [F3] to obtain the local map $A\to B$ and its common completion $T=\mathbb C[[w_1,\ldots,w_n]]/I\mathbb C[[w_1,\ldots,w_n]]$. [given, F1, F2, F3]
+
+2.1 The completion maps $A\to T$ and $B\to T$ are faithfully flat by [F4]. A faithfully flat map is injective: if $a\ne0$ were in its kernel, the nonzero ideal $(a)$ would remain nonzero after tensoring by faithfulness, while flatness would make $(a)\otimes T\to T$ injective; this map is zero because $a$ maps to zero. Hence $A\to T$ is injective. If an element of $A$ maps to zero in $B$, its image in $T$ is zero as well, so injectivity of $A\to T$ makes it zero. Therefore $A\to B$ is injective. [F4, F5, step 1.2, algebra]
