1.1 ["A1","F7","F8","F9","F10","construct"] AC implies DC: for a nonempty set $Y$ and a serial relation $R\subseteq Y\times Y$, the family of successor sets $\{\{z\in Y:yRz\}:y\in Y\}$ consists of nonempty sets. [A1] gives a choice function on this family; composing it with the successor-set map gives a self-map $s:Y\to Y$ with $yRs(y)$. For any prescribed $y_0$, [F9] gives the sequence $y_{k+1}=s(y_k)$. For any module $N$, the free module on its underlying set maps surjectively to $N$ by sending each standard basis vector to the element it names, using [F10]. Such a free module is projective: given a surjection $E\to Q$ and a map from the free module to $Q$, [A1] chooses in $E$ a preimage of the image of each basis vector, and [F10] extends that assignment to a lift. Applying this construction to each successive kernel and using [F9] to recurse gives a projective resolution. Thus the DC and resolution hypotheses of [F7] and [F8] hold. [A1, F7, F8, F9, F10, construct]
1.2 ["given","F1","F2","F2a","step 1.1","F3"] By [F1], around $x$ the scheme has a finite-type affine chart; [F2] and [F2a], whose dependent-choice hypothesis is supplied by step 1.1, make its polynomial defining ideal $I$ finitely generated. Translate the coordinates to put $x$ at the origin, adding a dummy coordinate with relation $w_1=0$ if the chart has no generators. Apply [F3] to obtain the local map $A\to B$ and its common completion $T=\mathbb C[[w_1,\ldots,w_n]]/I\mathbb C[[w_1,\ldots,w_n]]$. [given, F1, F2, F2a, step 1.1, F3]
2.1 ["A1","F4","F5","step 1.2","algebra"] By [F4] under [A1], the completion maps $A\to T$ and $B\to T$ are faithfully flat. For a nonzero $a\in A$, the ideal $(a)$ is a nonzero $A$-module. Faithfulness means $(a)\otimes_A T\ne0$: otherwise the nonexact sequence $0\to(a)\to0$ would become exact after tensoring. Flatness makes the multiplication map $(a)\otimes_A T\to A\otimes_A T=T$ injective. If $a$ mapped to zero in $T$, this map would be zero, a contradiction. Hence $A\to T$ is injective. If an element of $A$ maps to zero in $B$, its image in $T$ is zero as well, so injectivity of $A\to T$ makes it zero. Therefore $A\to B$ is injective. [A1, F4, F5, step 1.2, algebra]
2.2 ["A1","F4","F5","step 1.2"] Let $U\hookrightarrow V$ be any injection of $A$-modules and let $K$ be the kernel of $B\otimes_A U\to B\otimes_A V$. Tensoring $0\to K\to B\otimes_A U\to B\otimes_A V$ over $B$ with $T$ preserves its kernel because $T$ is flat over $B$ by [F4] under [A1]. By associativity, the resulting map is $T\otimes_A U\to T\otimes_A V$, which is injective because $T$ is flat over $A$. Thus $K\otimes_B T=0$; faithful flatness of $B\to T$ gives $K=0$. The flatness criterion [F5] now makes $B$ flat over $A$. [A1, F4, F5, step 1.2]
3.1 ["F3","F6","step 2.2"] The map $A\to B$ is local by [F3]. If $J\subsetneq A$ is a proper ideal, then $J$ lies in the maximal ideal of the local ring $A$, whose image lies in the maximal ideal of $B$; hence $JB\subsetneq B$. Combining this with the flatness in step 2.2 and [F6] shows that $A\to B$ is faithfully flat. [F3, F6, step 2.2]
3.2 ["F6","step 2.2","step 3.1","algebra"] Let $M$ be any $A$-module and $m\in M$ nonzero. Its annihilator $J=\{a\in A:am=0\}$ is a proper ideal, and the map $A/J\to M$ sending $1+J$ to $m$ is injective. Flatness from step 2.2 preserves this injection. The balanced tensor relations give inverse maps $(A/J)\otimes_A B\to B/JB$, $(a+J)\otimes b\mapsto ab+JB$, and $B/JB\to(A/J)\otimes_A B$, $b+JB\mapsto(1+J)\otimes b$. By [F6], $B/JB\ne0$, so $m\otimes1\ne0$. Therefore $M\to M\otimes_A B$ is injective for every $M$. [F6, step 2.2, step 3.1, algebra]
4.1 ["F7","F8","step 1.1","step 2.2","step 3.2"] Put $C=B/A$, which is defined because step 2.1 proves $A\hookrightarrow B$. For any left $A$-module $M$, apply [F7] under the DC and resolution construction in step 1.1 to the short exact sequence of right $A$-modules $0\to A\to B\to C\to0$. The long exact sequence gives $\operatorname{Tor}_1^A(A,M)\to\operatorname{Tor}_1^A(B,M)\to\operatorname{Tor}_1^A(C,M)\to A\otimes_A M\to B\otimes_A M$. The first two Tor groups vanish: $A$ is free over itself and $B$ is flat by step 2.2. The final map is injective by step 3.2, so exactness forces $\operatorname{Tor}_1^A(C,M)=0$ for every $M$. By [F8], $C=B/A$ is a flat $A$-module. [F7, F8, step 1.1, step 2.2, step 3.2] ∎

